{
  "dom_id": "step-q343491",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948186/assets/c63dfe563c07b143bdc7cc28ab4d1142bea8c455be31ce924fa310c5e4903305.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circular disk above is changing as the circle expands. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r,$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference.\n\nThe rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference $C$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ into the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
